A semi-circle of radius 8 cm, rocks back and forth along a line.  The distance between the line on which the semi-circle sits and the line above is 12 cm.  As it rocks without slipping, the semi-circle touches the line above at two points.  (When the semi-circle hits the line above, it immediately rocks back in the other direction.)  What is the distance between these two points, in millimetres, rounded off to the nearest whole number? [asy]

draw((-15, -8)--(15, -8));draw((-15, 4)--(15, 4));draw((-8, 0)--(8, 0){down}..{up}(-8, 0));

[/asy] (Note: After finding the exact value of the desired distance, you may find a calculator useful to round this value off to the nearest whole number.)

output:
When the semi-circle touches the horizontal line it is 12 cm above the line. With the distance between the lines being 12 cm the diameter of the semi-circle is now 8+12=20. However, so long as it is touching the line on one side it's radius is always a constant 8 cm. So if we draw a diameral line all the way across the line that the semi-circle is currently touching it this would make a triangle of base 8 cm with a height of 12 cm [asy]
draw((-12, 6)--(12, 6));draw((-10.8, 6)--(10.8, 6));draw Swipe((12, 6),linewidth(.15mm));
draw((-1.2, 6)--(11.8, 6));draw((-10.6, 6)--(10.8, 6));
draw((-10.6, 3)--(10.8, 3));draw((-10.8, 3)--(10.8, 3));
draw((-0.8, 0)--(0, 0));draw((-0.8, 12)--(0, 12));
[/asy]</think>
 So the length of the sequence of the points the semi-circle touches from are, in order, 4, -4, 4, -4, 4, -4, 4, -4, 4, -4, 4, -4, 4, -4. In other words, a sequence that goes from 0 to 16 by stepping -4 all and then 16 backwards. So we have a net forward movement of 2(16)=32 cm, and nowhere near 80 so our answer is $320\approx \boxed{320}$. The answer is [boxed{320}] millimetres. </answer>

