In general, when a problem asks for the total number of possibilities in a series of events, and there's more than one way for the events to happen, we use the Product Rule, which tells us that the total number of possibilities is the product, or multiplication, of the number of ways the events may happen. (So my specific question here is: "how many ways can the white balls be drawn, and how many ways can the red balls be drawn?")
Using the fact that order doesn't matter in this particular drawing, I know the white balls can be drawn in, say, 10 different ways, and the red balls can be drawn in, say, 5.
Now, using the Product Rule, I know the total number of possible draws is the product of the ways I can draw the white balls (10), and the ways I can draw the red balls (5), a product of $$
        \begin{pmatrix}
        5 \\
     10 \\
        \end{pmatrix}{\times}{\left(
        \begin{pmatrix}
        70 \\
        20 \\
        \end{pmatrix}
        \begin{pmatrix}
        \end{pmatrix}
        \begin{pmatrix}
        30 \\
        3 \\
        \end{pmatrix}
        \begin{pmatrix}
        \end{pmatrix}
        \end{pmatrix}}
        $$
where <pmatrix> <sub> r </sub> is n <sup>choose</sup> <sub> r </sub>.  Note, however, that the first number, "5," is the combination of 70 taken 10, then the product of 30 taken 3 = (5) < sports balls > 70 </sub> taken 10, then the product of 30 taken 3 = < sports balls > 70 </sub> < sports balls > 30 </sub> taken 3 </sub> taken 3. <end think>
